// std.gc: two hints about when the collector runs (Stage 29 W1c2, D305).
//
// Most programs need neither: the collector sizes its young generation
// from what survives. A program that builds a large structure which
// stays alive while it is built, and then drops it whole, knows two
// things the collector cannot:
//
//   gc.hold { ... }  no collection this thread would start runs while the
//                    block runs; its allocations still count (a hold whose
//                    own allocations pass AVANT_HEAP_MAX_MB stops with
//                    "heap limit in a no-GC region"), and a collection
//                    that falls due inside runs when the block ends, on
//                    every way out of it (return, break, continue, `?`).
//                    Nested holds are one hold. Other threads still
//                    collect (they may stop this one at a safepoint).
//   gc.collect()     a young collection now: what the young generation
//                    holds that nothing reaches is reclaimed; inside a
//                    hold it runs at the hold's end.
//
// Both are compiler forms (checker/visibility.av, mir/lower_ffi.av) on
// the runtime's avant_gc_hold_begin/end and avant_nursery_reclaim
// (runtime/avant_gc_collect.c). Importing this module is what makes
// `gc` name them; it declares nothing else. Under AVANT_GC_STRESS=1 a
// hold is inactive, so stress runs check the code inside it as they
// check any other.
